{{#> modal overlayId="siteswap-basics-overlay" modalId="siteswap-basics-modal" title="Siteswap basics"}}
    <p>
        "Siteswap" is sort of a nerdy, numerical way to describe juggling patterns. It's helpful because it simplifies juggling to the bare essentials: how high is each throw, and when does it land.
    </p>
    <p>
        The basic idea is to map numbers to throw heights: the higher the number, the higher the throw. If the number is odd, it means a throw from one hand to the other. If it's even, it means the ball lands in the same hand that threw it.
    </p>
    <p>
        This video by Taylor Glenn, on her YouTube channel <i>Taylor Tries</i>, is a great explainer:
        <div style="text-align:center; margin: 1.5em 0;">
            <iframe width="100%"
                src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/NsIlQDhMKro"
                title="YouTube video player"
                frameborder="0"
                allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
                style="aspect-ratio: 16 / 9"
                allowfullscreen>
            </iframe>
        </div>
    </p>
{{/modal}}
